(
    backstory: "Sirius was never a patient student. The elven war-colleges that raised him prize careful study, decades of measured incantation before a single bolt of force is loosed in anger, and Sirius found that tradition insufferable. He learned faster by testing his spells against real opposition — bandits, wild beasts, the occasional overconfident rival — than by copying diagrams in a candlelit scriptorium, and his tutors eventually gave up trying to slow him down.\n\nThat impatience earned him a reputation among the elder mages as reckless, even dangerous, and Sirius has made something of a game out of living up to it. He delights in the moment an opponent realizes, too late, that the young elf across from them is not merely posturing. There is little cruelty in it, more the satisfaction of a craftsman whose tools finally work exactly as intended, but he rarely troubles himself to explain the difference to those on the receiving end of his lightning.\n\nHe travels now less out of any grand purpose than restlessness — a hunger to find spellcasters and monsters worth the effort of a real duel, and perhaps, though he would never admit it aloud, a quiet wish to be taken seriously by the colleges that dismissed him. Sirius fights first and philosophizes later, if at all.",
    profile: (
        title: "The Merciless Spark",
        archetype: "Reckless Prodigy",
        core_motivation: "To prove his impatient, combat-tested magic superior to his tutors' caution",
        combat_style: "Aggressive offensive spellcasting, direct damage bolts, eager to duel",
    ),
)
